  "query_preview": "..\nA passing trough is producing isolated to scattered showers over the\nMarianas. This trough has some upper-level support as the remains of\nan upper-level low is trailing behind. There is a bit of uncertainty\nof how much support this low is giving due to the location compared \nto the trough is. If this trough slows down showers may become more \nconvectively active, however if the trough maintains or increases in \nforward motion connectivity may become stifled.\n\nLooking forwards, a dry pattern is expected through the beginning of\nnext week. \n\nMarine...\nBenign seas are expected through the weekend as combined seas are\nexpected be 3 to 4 feet falling to 2 to 3 feet over the next few\ndays. Winds are expected to be light to gentle. \n\nEastern Micronesia...\nThe calm weather continues across all of the region with light winds\nand just isolated showers. The models do hint at the possibility of a\nwetter pattern developing towards the end of the forecast period\n(late in the weekend), but we still have plenty of time to watch the\nmodel trends and satellite analysis until then. Mariners can expect\nlight to gentle winds and seas of 3 to 5 feet.\n\nWestern Micronesia...\nSatellite imagery shows isolated showers across the region. \nAltimetry shows combined seas between 2 and 5 feet.\n\nOnly minor changes were made with this forecast. Shower coverage \nremains isolated across the region, but scattered showers are \nexpected across Palau, Yap Proper, and Weno overnight. This drier \npattern looks to continue through much of the period with isolated \nto low-end (20 - 30%) scattered showers expected. The one exception \nis for  Friday at Palau with slightly higher PoPs (40%) expected, \ndue to an increase in low-level convergence from a sharp trough \ncurrently located across southern Chuuk State. \n\nNo changes were made to the marine forecast. Benign seas of 2 to 5 \nfeet are expected through the period. Seas may fall to a light swell \nfor Palau late this week.",
